1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,923
In the summer of 2006, during the World

2
00:00:02,923 --> 00:00:05,776
Cup in Germany, doctors in Munich noticed

3
00:00:05,776 --> 00:00:07,137
something strange.

4
00:00:07,787 --> 00:00:10,130
On days when the German national team

5
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:12,590
played, cardiac emergencies among local

6
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:15,226
fans were more than two and a half times

7
00:00:15,226 --> 00:00:16,514
higher than normal.

8
00:00:17,164 --> 00:00:18,519
People weren't running.

9
00:00:18,519 --> 00:00:19,770
They weren't playing.

10
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:21,020
They were watching.

11
00:00:21,670 --> 00:00:23,571
And watching was enough to push their

12
00:00:23,571 --> 00:00:24,663
hearts to the limit.

13
00:00:25,313 --> 00:00:27,476
Every weekend, billions of people do

14
00:00:27,476 --> 00:00:29,917
something that makes very little sense on

15
00:00:29,917 --> 00:00:30,416
paper:

16
00:00:31,066 --> 00:00:33,462
they spend hours watching strangers chase

17
00:00:33,462 --> 00:00:34,061
a ball —

18
00:00:34,711 --> 00:00:37,301
and feel joy, rage and heartbreak as if

19
00:00:37,301 --> 00:00:39,397
their own lives depended on it.

20
00:00:40,047 --> 00:00:40,610
Why?

21
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:43,572
If you like finding out why we do the

22
00:00:43,572 --> 00:00:45,710
strange things we do, subscribe.

23
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:47,675
It genuinely helps the channel.

24
00:00:48,325 --> 00:00:50,737
Watching sports is not a modern habit.

25
00:00:51,387 --> 00:00:55,275
The ancient Olympic Games began in Greece,

26
00:00:55,275 --> 00:00:58,127
traditionally dated to 776 BC.

27
00:00:58,777 --> 00:01:00,747
Crowds traveled for days to watch.

28
00:01:01,397 --> 00:01:03,973
In Rome, the Circus Maximus could hold

29
00:01:03,973 --> 00:01:06,613
well over a hundred thousand spectators

30
00:01:06,613 --> 00:01:07,933
for chariot races.

31
00:01:08,583 --> 00:01:11,153
And in Constantinople, today's Istanbul,

32
00:01:11,803 --> 00:01:14,272
chariot-racing fans were divided into

33
00:01:14,272 --> 00:01:16,001
teams named after colors:

34
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:17,976
the Blues and the Greens.

35
00:01:18,626 --> 00:01:21,346
In the year 532, the rival fans did

36
00:01:21,346 --> 00:01:23,136
something unthinkable:

37
00:01:23,136 --> 00:01:25,641
they united against the emperor.

38
00:01:26,291 --> 00:01:28,497
The Nika riots burned much of the city.

39
00:01:29,147 --> 00:01:31,447
According to the historian Procopius,

40
00:01:31,447 --> 00:01:33,977
around thirty thousand people were killed

41
00:01:33,977 --> 00:01:36,219
when the uprising was crushed in the

42
00:01:36,219 --> 00:01:37,024
Hippodrome.

43
00:01:37,674 --> 00:01:40,216
Sports fandom almost toppled one of the

44
00:01:40,216 --> 00:01:42,396
most powerful empires in history.

45
00:01:43,046 --> 00:01:45,271
So what happens inside us when we watch?

46
00:01:45,921 --> 00:01:49,051
In the 1990s, Italian scientists studying

47
00:01:49,051 --> 00:01:52,253
monkeys found neurons that fired both when

48
00:01:52,253 --> 00:01:54,246
a monkey grabbed a peanut

49
00:01:54,896 --> 00:01:56,735
and when it simply watched someone else

50
00:01:56,735 --> 00:01:57,237
grab one.

51
00:01:57,887 --> 00:01:59,555
They called them mirror neurons.

52
00:02:00,205 --> 00:02:02,703
How exactly this works in humans is still

53
00:02:02,703 --> 00:02:03,328
debated.

54
00:02:03,978 --> 00:02:06,387
But brain scans show that watching an

55
00:02:06,387 --> 00:02:09,097
action activates some of the same areas we

56
00:02:09,097 --> 00:02:10,362
use to perform it.

57
00:02:11,012 --> 00:02:12,753
And expertise makes it stronger.

58
00:02:13,403 --> 00:02:15,652
In one study, professional basketball

59
00:02:15,652 --> 00:02:17,790
players watched short clips of free

60
00:02:17,790 --> 00:02:18,352
throws.

61
00:02:19,002 --> 00:02:21,054
They could predict whether the ball would

62
00:02:21,054 --> 00:02:22,920
go in — before it left the shooter's

63
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:23,386
hand —

64
00:02:24,036 --> 00:02:25,984
far better than coaches or sports

65
00:02:25,984 --> 00:02:26,796
journalists.

66
00:02:27,446 --> 00:02:29,600
When you watch a striker shoot, a small

67
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,550
part of your brain is shooting too.

68
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:34,284
But mirroring alone doesn't explain the

69
00:02:34,284 --> 00:02:34,929
obsession.

70
00:02:35,579 --> 00:02:37,307
The real hook is something else:

71
00:02:37,307 --> 00:02:38,838
we don't know how it ends.

72
00:02:39,488 --> 00:02:42,033
Neuroscientist Wolfram Schultz found

73
00:02:42,033 --> 00:02:44,640
that dopamine neurons don't simply fire

74
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:46,130
when we get a reward.

75
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:49,094
They fire most strongly when something is

76
00:02:49,094 --> 00:02:50,619
better than we expected.

77
00:02:51,269 --> 00:02:53,703
When a reward is completely predictable,

78
00:02:53,703 --> 00:02:54,949
the response fades.

79
00:02:55,599 --> 00:02:58,035
That's why a recorded match whose result

80
00:02:58,035 --> 00:02:59,791
you already know feels flat.

81
00:03:00,441 --> 00:03:02,973
And it's why a last-minute goal feels like

82
00:03:02,973 --> 00:03:03,874
an explosion.

83
00:03:04,524 --> 00:03:06,853
Economists noticed this too.

84
00:03:06,853 --> 00:03:10,008
In 1956, Simon Rottenberg argued that

85
00:03:10,008 --> 00:03:13,238
fans want competition to be uncertain.

86
00:03:13,888 --> 00:03:16,113
If one team always wins, people stop

87
00:03:16,113 --> 00:03:16,797
watching.

88
00:03:17,447 --> 00:03:20,140
That's why many leagues use drafts, salary

89
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:21,816
caps and revenue sharing:

90
00:03:21,816 --> 00:03:23,612
to keep the ending unknown.

91
00:03:24,262 --> 00:03:26,774
Sport is one of the few places left where

92
00:03:26,774 --> 00:03:29,172
nobody — not even the richest person on

93
00:03:29,172 --> 00:03:31,513
Earth — can buy the ending in advance.

94
00:03:32,163 --> 00:03:34,168
Now here's the strangest part.

95
00:03:34,168 --> 00:03:35,809
Listen to how fans talk.

96
00:03:36,459 --> 00:03:38,519
When their team wins, they say "we won."

97
00:03:39,169 --> 00:03:40,971
When it loses, they often say "they

98
00:03:40,971 --> 00:03:41,433
lost."

99
00:03:42,083 --> 00:03:44,881
In 1976, psychologist Robert Cialdini

100
00:03:44,881 --> 00:03:47,413
and his colleagues counted how many

101
00:03:47,413 --> 00:03:50,145
students at American universities wore

102
00:03:50,145 --> 00:03:53,010
their school's clothing on Mondays after

103
00:03:53,010 --> 00:03:54,209
football games.

104
00:03:54,859 --> 00:03:57,278
After a win, significantly more students

105
00:03:57,278 --> 00:03:58,516
wore school colors.

106
00:03:59,166 --> 00:04:01,861
Asked to describe the game, they were more

107
00:04:01,861 --> 00:04:04,377
likely to say "we" after a victory, and

108
00:04:04,377 --> 00:04:05,874
"they" after a defeat.

109
00:04:06,524 --> 00:04:08,829
Cialdini called it basking in reflected

110
00:04:08,829 --> 00:04:09,365
glory.

111
00:04:10,015 --> 00:04:12,134
Later researchers named the opposite

112
00:04:12,134 --> 00:04:14,035
cutting off reflected failure.

113
00:04:14,685 --> 00:04:16,674
We borrow a little of the winners'

114
00:04:16,674 --> 00:04:18,931
success, and quietly step away from the

115
00:04:18,931 --> 00:04:19,468
losers.

116
00:04:20,118 --> 00:04:21,929
We didn't kick a single ball.

117
00:04:21,929 --> 00:04:24,363
But our self-esteem rises and falls with

118
00:04:24,363 --> 00:04:25,098
the score.

119
00:04:25,748 --> 00:04:27,806
Why would our self-esteem be tied to a

120
00:04:27,806 --> 00:04:28,559
team at all?

121
00:04:29,209 --> 00:04:32,494
In the early 1970s, psychologist Henri

122
00:04:32,494 --> 00:04:35,231
Tajfel ran a famous experiment.

123
00:04:35,881 --> 00:04:38,496
He divided boys into two groups based on

124
00:04:38,496 --> 00:04:40,989
something trivial, like which abstract

125
00:04:40,989 --> 00:04:43,239
painter they supposedly preferred,

126
00:04:43,889 --> 00:04:45,127
Klee or Kandinsky.

127
00:04:45,777 --> 00:04:47,424
They never met their group.

128
00:04:47,424 --> 00:04:49,840
There was no history, no rivalry, nothing

129
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:50,499
at stake.

130
00:04:51,149 --> 00:04:53,549
And yet, when asked to share out rewards,

131
00:04:53,549 --> 00:04:55,513
the boys favored their own group,

132
00:04:56,163 --> 00:04:58,522
often even if it meant everyone got less

133
00:04:58,522 --> 00:05:00,825
overall, just so their group could come

134
00:05:00,825 --> 00:05:01,538
out ahead.

135
00:05:02,188 --> 00:05:04,783
Tajfel and John Turner developed social

136
00:05:04,783 --> 00:05:05,937
identity theory:

137
00:05:06,587 --> 00:05:08,923
part of who we are comes from the groups

138
00:05:08,923 --> 00:05:11,097
we belong to. Our minds are built for

139
00:05:11,097 --> 00:05:11,640
tribes.

140
00:05:12,290 --> 00:05:14,711
For most of human history, belonging to a

141
00:05:14,711 --> 00:05:16,031
group meant survival.

142
00:05:16,681 --> 00:05:19,503
Sports gives that ancient instinct a home:

143
00:05:19,503 --> 00:05:22,137
colors, chants, rivals, and a reason to

144
00:05:22,137 --> 00:05:24,521
feel like part of something bigger.

145
00:05:25,171 --> 00:05:26,785
And it's not just in our heads.

146
00:05:27,435 --> 00:05:30,760
A 1998 study found that fans' testosterone

147
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:33,864
levels rose after their team won, and

148
00:05:33,864 --> 00:05:35,563
fell after a loss,

149
00:05:36,213 --> 00:05:38,206
almost as if they had fought the battle

150
00:05:38,206 --> 00:05:38,870
themselves.

151
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:41,533
Tribes don't just feel differently.

152
00:05:41,533 --> 00:05:42,910
They see differently.

153
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:46,577
In 1951, Princeton and Dartmouth played a

154
00:05:46,577 --> 00:05:48,840
brutal American football game.

155
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:51,446
Players were injured on both sides.

156
00:05:52,096 --> 00:05:54,751
Psychologists Albert Hastorf and Hadley

157
00:05:54,751 --> 00:05:57,111
Cantril showed a film of the game to

158
00:05:57,111 --> 00:05:59,116
students from both universities

159
00:05:59,766 --> 00:06:01,472
and asked them to count the rule

160
00:06:01,472 --> 00:06:02,154
violations.

161
00:06:02,804 --> 00:06:05,252
Princeton students saw Dartmouth commit

162
00:06:05,252 --> 00:06:07,641
about twice as many fouls as Dartmouth

163
00:06:07,641 --> 00:06:08,573
students did.

164
00:06:09,223 --> 00:06:11,010
Same film. Same plays.

165
00:06:11,010 --> 00:06:13,441
Two completely different games.

166
00:06:14,091 --> 00:06:15,773
Their paper was called "They Saw a

167
00:06:15,773 --> 00:06:16,216
Game."

168
00:06:16,866 --> 00:06:19,103
It's one of the reasons every fan is

169
00:06:19,103 --> 00:06:21,569
absolutely certain the referee is biased

170
00:06:21,569 --> 00:06:22,945
against their team.

171
00:06:23,595 --> 00:06:26,161
Sociologists Norbert Elias and Eric

172
00:06:26,161 --> 00:06:28,727
Dunning had a theory about why all of

173
00:06:28,727 --> 00:06:31,480
this matters so much in the modern world.

174
00:06:32,130 --> 00:06:34,759
Modern life, they argued, asks us to

175
00:06:34,759 --> 00:06:37,725
control our emotions almost all the time:

176
00:06:37,725 --> 00:06:40,017
at work, in traffic, in public.

177
00:06:40,667 --> 00:06:43,389
We rarely get to scream, hug strangers, or

178
00:06:43,389 --> 00:06:44,236
cry openly.

179
00:06:44,886 --> 00:06:47,428
Sport creates a protected space where

180
00:06:47,428 --> 00:06:49,334
intense emotion is allowed:

181
00:06:49,334 --> 00:06:51,494
a controlled loss of control.

182
00:06:52,144 --> 00:06:53,634
The danger is pretend.

183
00:06:53,634 --> 00:06:55,124
The feelings are real.

184
00:06:55,774 --> 00:06:57,913
A match is a story with heroes and

185
00:06:57,913 --> 00:07:00,282
villains, a beginning, a middle and an

186
00:07:00,282 --> 00:07:00,686
end,

187
00:07:01,336 --> 00:07:03,741
played out live, with nobody knowing the

188
00:07:03,741 --> 00:07:04,524
final page.

189
00:07:05,174 --> 00:07:07,395
It's a safe war, between tribes that go

190
00:07:07,395 --> 00:07:08,346
home afterward.

191
00:07:08,996 --> 00:07:11,888
Try this. Next time you watch a match, pay

192
00:07:11,888 --> 00:07:13,815
attention to one tiny word:

193
00:07:13,815 --> 00:07:14,457
"we."

194
00:07:15,107 --> 00:07:17,176
Notice when you say it, and when you stop

195
00:07:17,176 --> 00:07:17,787
saying it.

196
00:07:18,437 --> 00:07:20,638
Notice how you feel about the referee.

197
00:07:20,638 --> 00:07:22,893
Notice your heart rate in the last five

198
00:07:22,893 --> 00:07:23,484
minutes.

199
00:07:24,134 --> 00:07:26,367
You'll be watching two games at once:

200
00:07:26,367 --> 00:07:28,769
the one on the field, and the one inside

201
00:07:28,769 --> 00:07:29,718
your own head.

202
00:07:30,368 --> 00:07:32,674
We love sport not because it matters, but

203
00:07:32,674 --> 00:07:33,932
because it doesn't,

204
00:07:34,582 --> 00:07:36,705
and yet it lets us feel everything as if

205
00:07:36,705 --> 00:07:37,198
it did.

206
00:07:37,848 --> 00:07:40,492
It gives us a tribe without a war, a story

207
00:07:40,492 --> 00:07:42,960
without a script, and a reason to hug a

208
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:43,665
stranger.

209
00:07:44,315 --> 00:07:46,535
Maybe that's why, three thousand years

210
00:07:46,535 --> 00:07:48,647
after Olympia, we're still watching.

